# Colour steps: guide_coloursteps

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`guide_coloursteps()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/guide_coloursteps.html), which
covers `guide_colorsteps()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's
code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names
what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`guide_coloursteps()`, or `guide_colorsteps()` spelled the American way, is the legend of a binned
colour or fill scale: a colour bar, as `guide_colourbar()` draws, but with each bin in one constant
colour rather than a gradient. It is the default guide for binned colour scales. Giggly draws it at
its defaults, `guide: :colorsteps`, the default guide of `Giggly.Plot.scale_fill_binned/2` and the
other binned colour and fill scales, which label the ends with the limits too for `show_limits:
true`, but takes none of its options (`guide_coloursteps()` with its `even.steps` option). A plot
is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The
setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws
a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`.

## The default for binned colours

R's `expand.grid()` makes a row for every combination of `X1` and `X2` from 1 to 10, and `value`
is their product.

```r
df <- expand.grid(X1 = 1:10, X2 = 1:10)
df$value <- df$X1 * df$X2

p <- ggplot(df, aes(X1, X2)) + geom_tile(aes(fill = value))

# Coloursteps guide is the default for binned colour scales
p + scale_fill_binned()
```

The cell builds the same table, `X1` changing fastest, as `expand.grid()` builds it.

```elixir
pairs = for x2 <- 1..10, x1 <- 1..10, do: {x1, x2}

df = %{
  "X1" => Enum.map(pairs, fn {x1, _x2} -> x1 end),
  "X2" => Enum.map(pairs, fn {_x1, x2} -> x2 end),
  "value" => Enum.map(pairs, fn {x1, x2} -> x1 * x2 end)
}

p =
  df
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :X1, y: :X2})
  |> Plot.geom_tile(%{fill: :value})

p
|> Plot.scale_fill_binned()
|> render.()
```

## Bins of equal size

```r
# By default each bin in the guide is the same size irrespectively of how
# their sizes relate in data space
p + scale_fill_binned(breaks = c(10, 25, 50))
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_fill_binned(breaks: [10, 25, 50])
|> render.()
```

## Bins in proportion

```r
# This can be changed with the `even.steps` argument
p + scale_fill_binned(
  breaks = c(10, 25, 50),
  guide = guide_coloursteps(even.steps = FALSE)
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `guide_coloursteps()` with its `even.steps` option, which
sizes each bin in the legend in proportion to its range in the data.

## Showing the limits

```r
# By default the limits is not shown, but this can be changed
p + scale_fill_binned(guide = guide_coloursteps(show.limits = TRUE))

# (can also be set in the scale)
p + scale_fill_binned(show.limits = TRUE)
```

Giggly draws the second plot, the scale's own `show_limits:` labelling the ends of the scale too.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_fill_binned(show_limits: true)
|> render.()
```

Not in Giggly yet: for the first plot, `guide_coloursteps()` with its `show.limits` option.